Z||000000||fieroman_5000||12-05-2001||08:29 PM||||What is the differance with the pushrod and hydrolic lifters? And perfomance wise? Which is better...iI know this is a dumb question sooo don't laugh. or else  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>Thanks in advance<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000001||California Kid||12-05-2001||09:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroman_5000:<BR><B>What is the differance with the pushrod and hydrolic lifters? And perfomance wise? Which is better...iI know this is a dumb question sooo don't laugh. or else   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> <P>Thanks in advance<P>Fieroman  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The lifter be it hydraulic or solid rides on the cam shaft, there is also whats known as a roller lifter (which rolls) for high performance (solid and hydraulic).<P>The pushrod is a metal tube with closed round ends (little hole in the middle for oil), it resides between the lifter and the rocker arm. There are also roller rockers for less resistance, better performance.<P>Performance wise the hydraulic roller lifter is best suited for street/some race applications. Not to many people using what knows as solid lifters that require adjustments now and then.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroman_5000||12-05-2001||09:35 PM||||Thanx...just checking to see if my memory served me correctly...does the hydrolic lifter have some sort of hydrolic fluid to do with it? seeing as it's not solid there must be some sort of liquid?<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||12-05-2001||09:39 PM||||It gets it's oil from oil gallies in the block casting where is goes into lifter, gets pressurised, pumped through the hollow rod, up to the rocker arm to lubricate assembly. Very precise machining hence the word "pumping up".<P>May have missed some of the very specific technicals, just trying to keep this simple to understand. Best to pick up a good engine book if you really want to understand how everything functions in an engine.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
